     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "{\"pair_id\": \"Khnemet|860753\", \"verdict\": \"different_name\", \"reasoning\": \"The transliteration Ꜣḫ-n=j-mw.t reads as the signs ꜣ, ḫ, n, j, m, w, t. In Khnemet, ḫ is represented by 'kh', n by 'n', m by 'm', and the final t by 't'. The j and w may be left out, and the 'e' vowels can stand for no sign. The initial ꜣ has no vowel letter before 'kh' in Khnemet, because the name begins with the consonant K. An ꜣ at the start of a name needs a vowel letter next to its one neighbour, so this ꜣ has nothing to represent it. The rule counts that as a mismatch, so the two are different names.\"}"
     }
